# display-ref-enhanced.script - the reference walk with SEMANTIC input.
#
# The mods-on candidate runs with core.display's widened projection, so a
# fixed screen coordinate no longer lands on the shaman: this variant asks
# for the entity and the world destination instead, which the attribution
# resolves in whatever projection is current. The controls keep the fixed
# coordinates the committed reference frames were captured with, because
# mods-off classic is exactly the projection those coordinates were
# measured in and semantic resolution needs the sprite attribution that
# only a drawn, hooked frame provides.
# display-ref.script - semantic shaman walk after level/camera readiness.
# Round 13 asserts fresh BODY evidence and confirms each dump before the next.
# Reference dumps remain anchored to turns 820 and 860.
#
# RUN IT PINNED. RECOMP_PIN_CLOCK=1.
#
# An unpinned run reaches a different point of the game every time, so a
# reference taken from one is a reference to that afternoon. impl-t6 measured
# that under the pin the menu frame is byte-identical between runs, which is
# what makes an exact reference possible at all.
#
# WHY THE DUMPS ARE ANCHORED TO TURNS AND NOT TO TIME
#
# A dump at a script-scheduled millisecond lands wherever the game happened to
# be. `await turn>=N` waits for the simulation itself, so the dump instant is
# the same simulation instant in every run.
#
# The anchors must still be future when armed. Level1's old pinned flyby
# dump was at turn 609; selection now asserts BODY readiness, as turns alone
# do not establish visibility. The camera/input timing follows Gate C.
# Like gate-c, the walk then waits for turn 780 before reference capture.
# The logs record actual reached turns so late selection cannot masquerade as
# an exact 820/860 reference. The display gate checks that provenance.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Menu entry follows level1. Gameplay selection waits for readiness; the
# two reference dump anchors remain unchanged.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# level1.script - boot, start mission 01, and play it.
#
# Run with `make recomp-smoke`. Every action goes in through the same host
# state the real keyboard and mouse feed, so this exercises the paths the game
# actually uses rather than a back door built for testing.
#
# HOW THE INPUT HAS TO ARRIVE, WHICH TOOK TWO FIXES TO GET RIGHT
#
# The front end reads DirectInput buffered data when it is told the state
# changed; it does not poll. Buffered events are differences, so:
#
#   * A move must carry a relative delta. A move reporting only a destination
#     produces no DIMOFS_X or DIMOFS_Y event and the game's own cursor never
#     moves.
#   * The game integrates those deltas into a pointer of its own that started
#     wherever it chose, so an absolute coordinate here is only a guess about
#     where that pointer already was. `moveby` sends raw relative motion, and a
#     delta larger than the screen pins the game's pointer to the top-left
#     corner. That corner is the one position a script can be sure of, and
#     every coordinate below is measured from it.
#   * A click must stay down long enough for the guest to look. Pressing and
#     releasing in one host turn leaves the button at the level it started
#     from, the shim's comparison finds no change, and both events are lost.
#     The runner holds it for 120 ms.
#
# WHERE THE COORDINATES COME FROM
#
#   * New Game at (320, 140) and the tutorial prompt's No at (320, 219): the
#     recovered menu layout in docs/MENU.md (005d9660 / 004c3290 put the first
#     action row at y 140, rows 30 apart) and docs/MENU_NEW_GAME.md (prompt top
#     159, Yes at the paragraph bottom, No 30 lower), both centred on x 320.
#   * The start control at (605, 448) and the level-01 world at (322, 235):
#     measured off a dump of the level-select screen, which is a solar system
#     with a wooden panel across the bottom. The panel's right-hand button is
#     what starts the mission; clicking the world itself does nothing.
#   * The dialogue's OK at (236, 148) and the people at (390, 212): measured off
#     a dump of the running level.

# WAIT FOR THE GAME, NOT FOR THE CLOCK
#
# This used to be `wait 16000`, and that is what made the run fail about one
# time in two. The front end is roughly eight seconds of loading, then the
# copyright screen for five, then a cross-fade into the menu, which a live
# trace put at 13.9 s - so a fixed 16 s left two seconds of margin, and every
# second added to startup since (the mod loader, Lua, the MIDI SoundFont) spent
# some of it. A click that lands before the menu is live dismisses whatever is
# on screen instead of choosing New Game, the run never leaves the front end,
# and the eight assertions about the level all fail at once with no texture and
# no 3D scene to show for it.
#
# WHY THE CLAIM HAS TO HOLD
#
# `picture` is the share of the last presented frame that is not black. The
# copyright screen settles at 0.960 - its black border and the white Bullfrog
# box - and the menu at 0.998, so 0.98 tells the two apart. But both screens
# FADE in, and a fade passes through every value on its way up: the copyright
# screen's own fade-in crosses 0.98 for a frame or two before settling below
# it. A bare `await picture>0.98` is answered there, five seconds early, and
# the run fails exactly as the fixed wait did - that is not a guess, it is what
# the first two versions of this fix did. `for 2000` requires the claim to
# stay true, which no fade does.
await picture>0.98 for 2000 within 60000
dump menu

# New Game, then answer No to the tutorial prompt.
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 400
moveby 320 140
wait 700
click left 320 140
wait 3000
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 400
moveby 320 219
wait 700
click left 320 219
wait 12000
dump levelselect

# Start mission 01 from the panel's button.
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 300
moveby 605 448
wait 900
click left 605 448
wait 8000
dump level_start

# Dismiss the opening dialogue.
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 300
moveby 236 148
wait 700
click left 236 148
wait 4000
dump level_playing
# A simulation turn confirms that the level has started.
await turn>=1 within 120000

# M1's first actions: select blue shaman 1815, then order it to walk,
# and check both against the game's own record rather than against pixels.
#
# WHY NOT PIXELS
#
# The level opens in a camera flyby, and while it runs every frame differs from
# the last whatever the player does - so "the picture changed" cannot tell a
# selected brave from an ignored click. The entity table can. It is 2000
# records of 179 bytes at 0x8e0428, laid out as games/populous/tests/entity_codec.hpp encodes
# it: flags at +12, kind at +42, state at +44, owner at +47, position at +61 as
# x, z and altitude words. A blue brave is owner 0, kind 1.
#
# THE FLYBY
#
# This waits a fixed 20 seconds for it, and says so rather than pretending to
# detect it: there is no camera-settle signal exposed to the host, and a
# frame-difference test cannot find one because the units keep animating after
# the camera stops.
wait 34000
dump flyby_over

# What the game thinks is on the map. The peek is the whole entity table's
# first record, printed and decoded, so a run that finds nothing to watch shows
# what it was looking at instead of only saying that it failed.
# Reuse Gate C's starting camera and settling interval. This coordinate alone
# does not prove visibility: assert fresh BODY attribution before each gesture.
# Do not insert the old turn-620 wait, which changed Gate C's selection timing.
camera 5386 55651
wait 800
peek 0x8e0428 179
watch 0 1

# Select shaman 1815, then order it to walk.
#
# BOTH GESTURES ARE LEFT CLICKS, WHICH A RUN HAD TO ESTABLISH
#
# A click on a brave selects it and a click on the ground sends it there. A
# right-click on the ground does nothing at all: the same script with the
# second click on the right button left every entity in the table exactly where
# it started, and with the left button the selected one walked two cells east.
#
# Which entity was clicked is not guessed from the pixels. A click on a brave
# writes byte +122 of that brave's record and of no other's - the person flag
# at 0x7a that docs/COMMANDS.md records the selection code setting - so the
# host follows whichever entity the game itself marked, and the click after
# that one is the order.
#
# NO CORNER PIN FROM HERE ON, WHICH COST A RUN TO FIND OUT
#
# In the level, holding the pointer against the edge of the screen scrolls the
# map - that is what the edge is for. So the corner pin that makes a menu
# coordinate certain moves the camera underneath every coordinate measured
# after it, and two dumps taken either side of one showed a different landscape.
# Inside the level the pointer is moved absolutely instead: `move` sends the
# difference from where the script last put it, the game integrates the same
# difference, and the two stay together as long as neither clamps - which is
# why every coordinate below is well inside the frame.
# Resolve the shaman's attributed body and level1's destination using the
# current projection, as in gate-c.script. Assertions use the click's exact
# fresh BODY evidence (a visible shadow is insufficient), and stop the script
# on timeout. Reassert after settling because the body may leave the frame.
await entity_body 1815 within 20000
move entity 1815
wait 800
await entity_body 1815 within 20000
click entity 1815
wait 1500
dump selected
move world 5376 55040 128
wait 800
click world 5376 55040 128
# gate-c anchors the completed walk here; fixed waits would spend reference
# headroom again after any time spent waiting for entity attribution.
await turn>=780 within 120000
dump ordered

# Reference frames for every arm, captured at the first completed gameplay
# present at turns 820 and 860, with unchanged frame names and provenance.
# Only one dumpat can be pending. Await each turn on the same metric, then
# confirm its firing before arming the next: a late turn await can pass in the
# same script tick without a present. dumpat_fired closes that gap and stops
# on timeout. Image-write completion is checked separately at smoke shutdown.
# Actual reached turns remain in logs; late anchors still fail the gate.
dumpat turn>=820 ref_t820
await turn>=820 within 120000
await dumpat_fired>=1 within 120000
dumpat turn>=860 ref_t860
await turn>=860 within 120000
await dumpat_fired>=2 within 120000

wait 6000
dump walking

# What has to be true by now.
expect textures>0
expect draws>0
expect writebacks>0
# The level fills the screen, unlike the solar system that precedes it, which
# is mostly black by design. The running level measures 1.00; half of that is
# the line below which something is badly wrong rather than a target.
expect scene_nonblack>0.50
expect present_nonblack>0.50
expect audio_peak>0.05
# A game that is running keeps changing what it shows. The present count alone
# cannot tell a running game from a frozen one presenting the same frame.
expect present_changes>200
expect presents>50

# And what the game's own record has to say. These are the assertions that
# distinguish a level being played from a level being looked at.
#
#   entities              blue braves the scan found on the map
#   watch_found           one of them is being followed
#   watch_selected        and it is the one the game marked as selected, not
#                         merely the first one in the table
#   watch_moved           how far it got, in world units, from where it was
#                         when the order was given. A cell is 512 units, so
#                         half a cell is well past any jitter; the run measures
#                         about 1145, which is two cells and a bit.
#   watch_toward_target   the share of samples after the order that did not
#                         take it further from where it ended up. A unit
#                         walking somewhere keeps getting closer to it; one
#                         milling about does not. The target is where it
#                         finished. The order now projects a fixed world
#                         destination, but this metric still measures progress
#                         toward the endpoint, not distance to that destination.
#   watch_state_changes   its state byte left the one it had when idle
expect entities>0
expect watch_found>0
expect watch_selected>0
expect watch_samples>100
expect watch_moved>256
expect watch_toward_target>0.95
expect watch_state_changes>0

quit
